1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,040
Listen to a short talk on biology.

2
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:05,262
Today, we are going to continue our discussion of social insects,

3
00:00:05,262 --> 00:00:06,980
focusing on the Argentine ant

4
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:11,120
which as you might guess is a species of ant that are natives to Argentina.

5
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:18,180
We'll consider what happened to this type of ant after some members of the species move to California from their original habitat.

6
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:24,230
Ok, well, in Argentina, these Argentine ants behave like most ant species around the world.

7
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:29,856
They fight other ants of the same species if those ants are from some other nests.

8
00:00:29,856 --> 00:00:33,020
But the Argentine ants living in California behave differently.

9
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:36,453
Ants from different nests form a single large colony.

10
00:00:36,453 --> 00:00:41,030
Within this colony, there is little aggression among ants from different nests.

11
00:00:41,030 --> 00:00:44,065
And when they fight insects from outside their colony,

12
00:00:44,065 --> 00:00:48,787
the Argentine ants can quickly recruit a huge army from their network of nests.

13
00:00:48,787 --> 00:00:52,160
This of course gives them advantages over other ants' species.

14
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:57,270
So then, why do Argentine ants behave differently in California than they do in Argentina?

15
00:00:57,270 --> 00:01:04,280
Well, using genetic testing, researches found that all the Argentine ants in California were very similar genetically.

16
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:08,440
You see, when the first Argentine ants came to California

17
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:10,196
their population must have been very small,

18
00:01:10,196 --> 00:01:16,112
and all the later generations of Argentine ants there must be descended from the same few ancestors.

19
00:01:16,112 --> 00:01:18,200
So they are all closely related.

20
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:21,800
This discovery is important, because for most social insects,

21
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:27,000
membership in a colony is based on how closely related they are genetically.
